    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Scholars like Bowles and Gintis argue that IQ and standardized testing reinforce existing social inequalities, as they favor those from more affluent backgrounds who have had better access to education and resources. The work of critical theorists like Pierre Bourdieu highlights how these measures perpetuate a narrow, Eurocentric definition of intelligence and ability, dismissing the value of non-academic forms of intelligence and cultural capital. Furthermore, research by scholars like James Flynn and Richard Lynn suggests that IQ scores are influenced by factors like nutrition, healthcare, and socio-economic status, rendering them a poor indicator of innate ability.",
